 7\\end{bmatrix}$.\nHowever, $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation $y=5+3x$ gives the following:\n$y|= 5 + 3x; 7|= 5 + 3(-1); 7|= 2\\cdot$\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf{0} \\not\\in L$, this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139534949 false] [17592186119423 1145 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/assets/8a5ed52cd0a1f16d2284a101a757e57156f76aa24e11a1d15cbfacaa7e5c77b6.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $y=5+3x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5+3x \\\\ (0) & =5+3(0) \\\\ 0 & =5\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/assets/9468a26323576c7ab92150c53c8cbc27cc719f91d096547f55d698643cf15658.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/assets/513426187d03c0ee2d650b57432c6370e74aa81ec87cdfe92490aec2780aa9cb.png) from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+(-1) \\\\ 5+2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/assets/38aed9478a8cb36691b5f05dbfe1fd0f9d8555ea43b94a21ee43e265d5a2afe1.png) since substituting the coordinates into the equation $y=5+3x$ gives the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5+3x \\\\ 7 & =5+3(-1) \\\\ 7 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf{0} \\not\\in L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139534949 true] [17592186119424 1100 "Let $L$ be the line in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ that passes through the points $(1,2,2)$ and $(2,1,1)$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n2. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n3. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n4. $L$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$" 13194139534949 false] [17592186119424 1100 "Let $L$ be the line in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ that passes through the points $(1,2,2)$ and $(2,1,1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- $L$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$" 13194139534949 true] [17592186119424 1145 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces:\n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The direction vector of the line $L$ is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n$r|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nfor $t\\in\\mathbb R$, and the corresponding Cartesian equation is\n${x-1}=\\dfrac{y-2}{-1}=\\dfrac{z-2}{-1}$.\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since\n${(0)-1}\\neq\\dfrac{(0)-2}{-1}=\\dfrac{(0)-2}{-1}. \\quad {\\color{red}\\times}$\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the vectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nHowever, the point $(3,3,3)$ does not belong to $L$ since\n${(3)-1}\\neq\\dfrac{(3)-2}{-1}=\\dfrac{(3)-2}{-1}. \\quad {\\color{red}\\times}$\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf{0} \\not\\in L$, this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139534949 false] [17592186119424 1145 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/assets/10865a16bf90e96dc3d6fb4897751a84b0db7a12c55843c73767f77e8817bc0f.png)\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in\\mathbb R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n{x-1}=\\dfrac{y-2}{-1}=\\dfrac{z-2}{-1}.\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since \n\n$$\n{(0)-1}\\neq\\dfrac{(0)-2}{-1}=\\dfrac{(0)-2}{-1}.  \\quad {\\color{red}\\times}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the vectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/assets/f55d965d26e9f36702c88b259e7f60c84762894dccf7a72c9429fe84727fd922.png)\nHowever, the point $(3,3,3)$ does not belong to $L$ since\n\n$$\n{(3)-1}\\neq\\dfrac{(3)-2}{-1}=\\dfrac{(3)-2}{-1}. \\quad {\\color{red}\\times}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf{0} \\not\\in L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139534949 true] [17592186119425 1100 "Let $\\Pi$ be the plane with equation $x+4y-3z=2$ in $\\mathbb{R}^3$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\Pi$ contains the zero-vector\n2. $\\Pi$ is closed under vector addition\n3. $\\Pi$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n4. $\\Pi$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$" 13194139534949 false] [17592186119425 1100 "Let $\\Pi$ be the plane with equation $x+4y-3z=2$ in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $\\Pi$ contains the zero-vector\n\n- $\\Pi$ is closed under vector addition\n\n- $\\Pi$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- $\\Pi$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$" 13194139534949 true] [17592186119425 1145 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces:\n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $\\Pi$ contains vectors of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The plane does not pass through the origin since\n$x + 4y - 3z|= 2; (0) + 4(0) - 3(0)|= 2; 0|= 2\\cdot$\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Then\n$a + b|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 + 1 \\\\ 0 + 1 \\\\ 0 + 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$,\nHowever, $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ does not belong to $\\Pi$ since\n$x + 4y - 3z|= 2; (3) + 4(1) - 3(1)|= 2; 4|= 2\\cdot$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf{0} \\not\\in \\Pi$, this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $\\Pi$ is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139534949 false] [17592186119425 1145 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $\\Pi$ contains vectors of the form \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/assets/ff70a23af671918e56523ccd14c7c52bcafe502b3d5656f4e7a3f47606eda810.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The plane does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4y-3z & =2 \\\\ (0)+4(0)-3(0) & =2 \\\\ 0 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/assets/d08091d57940e8ef91dc371a628eae1c1dc7395cf2d284f48915593116a59510.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/assets/3117536c9d0c6d9e228d909405e8542abdc50dfda5cef00e92719086d795855f.png) Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2+1 \\\\ 0+1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ does not belong to $\\Pi$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4y-3z & =2 \\\\ (3)+4(1)-3(1) & =2 \\\\ 4 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf{0} \\not\\in \\Pi,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $\\Pi$ is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139534949 true] [17592186119847 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321861 13194139534949 true] [17592186119847 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321868 13194139534949 true] [17592186119847 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321875 13194139534949 true] [17592186119847 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321882 13194139534949 true] [17592186119847 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321903 13194139534949 true] [17592186119848 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321889 13194139534949 true] [17592186119848 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321910 13194139534949 true] [17592186119848 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321917 13194139534949 true] [17592186119848 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321924 13194139534949 true] [17592186119849 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321896 13194139534949 true] [17592186119849 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321931 13194139534949 true] [17592186119849 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321938 13194139534949 true] [17592186119849 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321945 13194139534949 true] [17592186120071 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534949 true] [17592186120071 1100 "Let $L$ be the line in $R^{3}$ that passes through the points $(-2, 2,-2)$ and $(3,-4, 2)$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n2. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n3. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n4. $L$ is **not** a subspace of $R^{3}$" 13194139534949 false] [17592186120071 1100 "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(-2,2,-2)$ and $(3,-4,2).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" 13194139534949 true] [17592186120071 1145 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{5}=\\frac{y-2}{-6}=\\frac{z+2}{4}.\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since \n\n$$\n\\frac{(0)+2}{5}\\ne \\frac{(0)-2}{-6}\\ne \\frac{(0)+2}{4}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the vectors $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHowever, the point $(1,-2,0)$ does not belong to $L$ since\n\n$$\n\\frac{(1)+2}{5}\\ne \\frac{(-2)-2}{-6}\\ne \\frac{(0)+2}{4}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139534949 true] [17592186120077 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534949 true] [17592186120077 1100 "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $x - y - z = 1$ in $R^{3}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n2. $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n3. $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n4. $Π$ is **not** a subspace of $R^{3}$" 13194139534949 false] [17592186120077 1100 "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $x-y-z=1$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n- $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n- $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" 13194139534949 true] [17592186120077 1145 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ x-y-1\\end{aligned}],\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The line does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x-y-1 \\\\ (0) & =(0)-(0)-(1) \\\\ 0 & =-1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ 0+0 \\\\ -1+0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ does not belong to $Π$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x-y-1 \\\\ (-1) & =(1)-(0)-1 \\\\ -1 & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin Π,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139534949 true] [17592186321861 1017 #edb/ref 17592186321862 13194139534949 true] [17592186321861 1089 "q-156596" 13194139534949 true] [17592186321861 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534949 true] [17592186321861 1100 "Let $L$ be the line with equation $y=-x$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n