ligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHowever, point $(4,4,4)$ does not belong to $L$ since it does not satisfy the equation of the line. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\ny=4\\ne 2.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139534949 true] [17592186321890 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321891 13194139534949 true] [17592186321890 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321892 13194139534949 true] [17592186321890 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321893 13194139534949 true] [17592186321890 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321894 13194139534949 true] [17592186321890 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321895 13194139534949 true] [17592186321890 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534949 true] [17592186321890 1041 "selection" 13194139534949 true] [17592186321890 1104 #edb/ref 17592186321894 13194139534949 true] [17592186321890 1110 #uuid "a5a6506c-7762-54ac-bb89-5cf016215df2" 13194139534949 true] [17592186321891 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534949 true] [17592186321891 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534949 true] [17592186321891 1111 #uuid "4cf3594e-92ec-5d64-a9e6-9cac25a04db2" 13194139534949 true] [17592186321892 1034 "I only" 13194139534949 true] [17592186321892 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534949 true] [17592186321892 1111 #uuid "c61fd597-5eb4-5da1-9b18-3df4344a4f67" 13194139534949 true] [17592186321893 1034 "III and IV only" 13194139534949 true] [17592186321893 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534949 true] [17592186321893 1111 #uuid "280d8717-17ca-5875-84c1-5c13383701dd" 13194139534949 true] [17592186321894 1034 "IV only" 13194139534949 true] [17592186321894 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534949 true] [17592186321894 1111 #uuid "b7816e7b-a719-5793-b0e8-6c58ea02da22" 13194139534949 true] [17592186321895 1034 "II only" 13194139534949 true] [17592186321895 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534949 true] [17592186321895 1111 #uuid "3cb84162-37be-5dcb-9d87-b46a2b8a0167" 13194139534949 true] [17592186321896 1017 #edb/ref 17592186321897 13194139534949 true] [17592186321896 1089 "q-156728" 13194139534949 true] [17592186321896 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534949 true] [17592186321896 1100 "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $z=2x-5y$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n- $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n- $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" 13194139534949 true] [17592186321896 1113 #uuid "b2fdea56-73c0-52eb-9e1b-09d0f326257e" 13194139534949 true] [17592186321896 1145 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ 2x-5y\\end{aligned}],\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $z=2x-5y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2x-5y \\\\ (0) & =2(0)-5(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-5{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ 2{b}_{1}-5{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-5{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ 2{b}_{1}-5{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ (2{a}_{1}-5{a}_{2})+(2{b}_{1}-5{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ 2({a}_{1}+{b}_{1})-5({a}_{2}+{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=2x-5y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-5{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-5{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k(2{a}_{1}-5{a}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ 2k{a}_{1}-5k{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=2x-5y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, the plane $Π$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" 13194139534949 true] [17592186321897 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321898 13194139534949 true] [17592186321897 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321899 13194139534949 true] [17592186321897 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321900 13194139534949 true] [17592186321897 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321901 13194139534949 true] [17592186321897 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321902 13194139534949 true] [17592186321897 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534949 true] [17592186321897 1041 "selection" 13194139534949 true] [17592186321897 1104 #edb/ref 17592186321901 13194139534949 true] [17592186321897 1110 #uuid "045c374a-2a1f-595f-bbd8-fce31044abbf" 13194139534949 true] [17592186321898 1034 "II and IV only" 13194139534949 true] [17592186321898 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534949 true] [17592186321898 1111 #uuid "9b139c47-a395-556a-87fd-157d13003b8f" 13194139534949 true] [17592186321899 1034 "I only" 13194139534949 true] [17592186321899 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534949 true] [17592186321899 1111 #uuid "366325f2-8db5-5d8c-bf28-a813fccbf802" 131941