y & =3x \\\\ 2(0) & =3(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a+\\frac{3}{2}b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}(a+b)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $2y=3x.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ k(\\frac{3}{2}a)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}(ka)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $2y=3x.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" 13194139534949 true] [17592186321904 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321905 13194139534949 true] [17592186321904 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321906 13194139534949 true] [17592186321904 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321907 13194139534949 true] [17592186321904 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321908 13194139534949 true] [17592186321904 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321909 13194139534949 true] [17592186321904 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534949 true] [17592186321904 1041 "selection" 13194139534949 true] [17592186321904 1104 #edb/ref 17592186321907 13194139534949 true] [17592186321904 1110 #uuid "9b49e240-e720-5f28-b066-c1115d63a18b" 13194139534949 true] [17592186321905 1034 "I and IV only" 13194139534949 true] [17592186321905 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534949 true] [17592186321905 1111 #uuid "6465787e-3b57-5d47-93cb-05f969206c7f