49 true] [17592186321945 1145 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3-y+2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The line does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y-2z & =3 \\\\ (0)+(0)-2(0) & =3 \\\\ 0 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider $a=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3+2 \\\\ 0+1 \\\\ 0+0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ does not belong to $Π$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y-2z & =3 \\\\ (5)+(1)-2(0) & =3 \\\\ 6 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin Π,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139534949 true] [17592186321946 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321947 13194139534949 true] [17592186321946 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321948 13194139534949 true] [17592186321946 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321949 13194139534949 true] [17592186321946 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321950 13194139534949 t